22:18:32 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It’s reasonable to notice a pattern in the people you personally know. That doesn’t mean you are inventing it—but it also does not establish that football causes poor academic performance or that players as a group are less intelligent. A few things can make that impression common: - Some students may prioritize sports over school, especially in highly competitive programs. - Football can consume a lot of time and energy, which can hurt grades without good planning. - The more visible or loud players may stand out in memory more than the quiet, academically focused ones. - A particular school’s team culture may reward “toughness” or discourage taking academics seriously. Those are concerns about the **specific team environment and time demands**, not about whether someone who plays football is inherently a “meathead.” Your cousin should look at the actual program: ask current players how they manage grades, check whether coaches enforce academic eligibility, see whether tutoring or study halls exist, and pay attention to whether teammates respect school effort. If joining would push him toward bad habits or away from his goals, that is a good reason to reconsider. If the team has a healthy culture and he can manage the workload, playing football and doing well academically are completely compatible.
