22:19:46 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Time and energy are limited, so there can be a real tradeoff **if** someone lets football crowd out studying, sleep, reading, or other mentally demanding activities. A player who spends every spare hour training and then is exhausted may perform worse in class. But that is not a general “strength versus intelligence” seesaw. Physical training does not inherently make someone less sharp; exercise often supports mood, sleep, attention, and cognition. Many athletes manage demanding training while succeeding academically because they structure their time, get support, and protect sleep. The football-specific concern is more concrete: - **Schedule and fatigue:** Practices, games, travel, and lifting can leave less time for coursework. - **Team culture:** If teammates and coaches mock studying or excuse bad grades, that can hurt outcomes. - **Head impacts:** Repeated impacts are a health risk that may affect cognition for some players. - **Sleep, nutrition, and substance use:** These can strongly affect both athletic and academic performance. So the right conclusion is not “football makes people unintelligent,” but: **a poorly managed football commitment—or unsafe exposure to head impacts—can interfere with academic performance and brain health.** Your cousin can reduce the non-injury risks by setting grade goals, planning study time, maintaining sleep, and leaving a program that pressures him to ignore school or symptoms.
