22:20:40 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Football culture often does emphasize toughness, physicality, and playing through discomfort—but that does not mean every team devalues academics, judgment, or player health. Programs differ a lot by school, age level, coach, league, and administration. The concern is strongest when “toughness” means things like: - mocking players for reporting concussion symptoms; - pressuring injured players to return; - treating schoolwork as unimportant; - glorifying reckless hits or hazing; - punishing players who prioritize sleep, medical care, or class. Those are red flags, not unavoidable features of every football team. A well-run program can value discipline, learning, safety, and academics while still being competitive and physically demanding. Your cousin should not assume the team is fine just because it is called a sports program—but he also should not assume it is anti-intellectual just because it plays football. He can talk to current and former players, ask about academic eligibility and support, attend a practice, and ask directly how suspected concussions and missed classes are handled.
