22:21:06 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Repeated head impacts are not “noise.” They are arguably the most serious football-specific concern, and they can affect cognition in some players—especially with concussions, inadequate recovery, or high cumulative exposure. But “one simple reason football players end up less sharp” overstates what the evidence can show. Brain outcomes vary widely, and researchers cannot infer from someone playing football that they have cognitive impairment or lower intelligence. Academic performance is also influenced by sleep, workload, support, stress, and team culture. A precise takeaway is: > Tackle football involves repeated head-impact exposure that can raise the risk of short- and long-term cognitive problems. Anyone considering it should weigh that risk seriously, use a program with strong safety practices, and report symptoms promptly. That is already a substantial criticism of the sport. It just does not justify assuming an individual player is unintelligent.
