22:21:20 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I would say the same core thing about both sports: repeated blows to the head can cause acute concussion and may raise the risk of longer-term problems with cognition, mood, and behavior. Boxing often involves intentionally targeting the head, so high-exposure boxers are a particularly serious concern. Tackle football also involves frequent head impacts, including impacts that may not produce an obvious concussion. What I would not say about either group is that every boxer or football player is unintelligent, or that participation proves someone has become cognitively impaired. Exposure, injury history, recovery, age of first exposure, years played, position, and individual factors all matter, and outcomes vary. For someone deciding whether to participate, the meaningful comparison is not “are participants dumb?” It is: **how much head-impact exposure am I accepting, what protections exist, and are the benefits worth the risk?** If your cousin wants to reduce that risk substantially, flag football or a non-contact sport is a reasonable alternative.
