22:23:48 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That sounds like a real pattern at your old school, and it would be reasonable to take it seriously when evaluating a similar program. It could reflect who chose football there, how the school sorted students, the team’s time demands, eligibility rules, coaching culture, or weak academic support. But even a striking pattern at one school does not establish that football itself makes players less academically capable, or that every team will look the same. It does give you concrete questions to ask about your cousin’s team: - What are players’ academic eligibility and graduation outcomes? - Are study halls, tutoring, or grade checks required? - Do coaches support students who need to miss activities for academic obligations? - How many practice, travel, and lifting hours are expected? - Do teachers report that players keep up with work? - Are there signs that the school funnels certain students into particular tracks or gives athletes different expectations? If the answers show the team consistently pulls students away from school or normalizes falling behind, that is a solid, non-stereotyped reason to advise your cousin against joining.
